{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Replacement/residual bug split from witted-invisible-roan after completion verification refused close on 2026-09-07.\n\nResidual defect:\nThe configured rebar MCP endpoint can still return persistent HTTP 502 after concurrent gate load if the MCP process is OOM-killed or otherwise dies while nginx's materialized single-server upstream still points at the dead backend. The parent landed detection, retire-sweep repair, memory instrumentation, and a reduced whole-store read footprint, but those do not prove the serving path cannot recur under concurrent gate load.\n\nVerifier blocker on parent, summarized:\n- nginx still has a single materialized upstream with no failover path;\n- memory oversubscription under concurrent gates is not bounded by admission control, instance sizing, or off-box execution;\n- existing comments identify the latency/read-scan issue (`lousy-unsuited-gnu` / `94c5-b42e-ac80-4f06`) as separate and not fixed by the memory reduction.\n\nDesign question to resolve here:\nChoose the prevention mechanism(s) for recurrence — e.g. MCP upstream failover/self-healing, gate admission/concurrency limits, moving gate execution off-box, or a data-backed capacity change — and implement enough that `/mcp` cannot remain pinned to a dead backend after gate-induced MCP failure.\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- [ ] The selected design is recorded with why alternatives were accepted/rejected.\n- [ ] A reproduced or simulated MCP backend death under gate-like load recovers without a persistent nginx 502.\n- [ ] The prevention is covered by tests through the relevant surface(s) and by an operator-visible metric/log/alarm.\n- [ ] Any capacity/concurrency number used by the prevention cites measured data, including or linking the AC5 campaign split to `inherited-americium-porpoise`.\n\n\n## RCA / design decision (2026-09-07)\n\nThis ticket is the remaining prevention gap after `witted-invisible-roan`: the current\nblue/green deploy path refuses to delete a crashed live MCP backend, but it only logs\n`mcp-live-backend-down` and waits for a source-changing redeploy or Docker restart. If the\ncontainer is exited/OOM-killed and Docker does not restart it promptly, nginx can stay pinned to\nthat dead single-server upstream and `/mcp` returns persistent 502.\n\nSelected prevention: add an autodeploy self-heal path that runs on every tick before ordinary\nsource-change deploy decisions. When the live upstream port belongs to a managed MCP container\nthat is not running, autodeploy must:\n\n1. keep the dead live container until after a replacement is promoted (no `rm -f` of the serving\n   reference);\n2. honor the existing MCP memory floor and two-port cap so healing cannot worsen the same\n   memory-pressure class;\n3. start a replacement from the currently deployed MCP image on the free blue/green port;\n4. require the same `/health` readiness, store-readiness, startup-handshake, nginx-test, and\n   nginx-reload gates as a normal MCP cutover;\n5. atomically flip nginx to the replacement, then reap the old exited backend and emit countable\n   journal markers (`AUTODEPLOY_MCP_SELF_HEAL` plus existing error markers on failure).\n\nRejected alternatives:\n\n- nginx multi-upstream/failover: would hide a dead backend only when a second healthy backend\n  already exists, but the existing design deliberately keeps a tiny bounded two-port pool and\n  retires old never-idle backends; it does not guarantee spare live capacity after load.\n- increasing instance size or only changing memory limits: AC4's measurement campaign is split\n  to `inherited-americium-porpoise`; capacity may still be useful, but it does not repair a\n  single-server nginx pointer after a process death.\n- moving gates off-box: larger architecture change, not needed for the immediate persistent-502\n  recurrence.\n\n## Testing / verification plan\n\n- RED test in `tests/scripts/test_autodeploy_mcp_bluegreen.py` simulates nginx pointing at an\n  exited managed MCP backend with no source change pending, and asserts the tick starts a\n  replacement, flips upstream to it, emits a self-heal marker, and only then reaps the dead\n  backend.\n- Companion test covers low-memory self-heal refusal: no replacement starts, upstream remains\n  unchanged, and the existing memory-abort marker is emitted.\n- Run `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" pytest -q tests/scripts/test_autodeploy_mcp_bluegreen.py`\n  and full `make verify` before push.\n- Live verification after merge: observe `/mcp/health` or container `/health`, and confirm no\n  persistent 502 after intentionally abandoning scratch test changes; no Terraform alarm apply is\n  required unless HCL changes emerge."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788834980958681001,"uuid":"d22b1876-3e53-4841-adc4-11ff03000c31"}